  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nCurrently have a weak upper impulse situated over IA with a deep \nupper low off of the Eastern Seaboard. Meanwhile, weak upper ridging \nis in place from the Central/Southern Rockies into the High Plains. \n\nStorms have been ongoing over southeast KS into eastern OK for the \nlast several hours. This is in an area of 850-700mb theta-e \nadvection and is also just ahead of a weak mid level trough. While \nthis activity may not remain this widespread, at least a few storms \nwill continue for at least the next 3-5 hours and possibly a \nbit longer over southeast KS as the mid level trough takes its \ntime sliding east. Even though we will be fairly unstable this \nafternoon with minimal capping, went ahead and kept pops out due\nto lack of decent focus or upper support. However, it's not \ncompletely out of the question a storm or two could develop \nmainly over the Flint Hills this afternoon.\n\nBy Monday morning there remains good model agreement in a deep \nupper low digging over Central/Southern CA with upper riding \nthrough the Plains. This will keep things dry across the area \nfor Mon into early Mon evening. Showers and storms will start to\noverspread western and central KS late Mon night into Tue \nmorning as upper diffluence starts to overspread the High Plains\nalong with low level jet ramping up. By Tue afternoon, upper \nlow will be digging over the Desert Southwest with what is left \nof Polo starting to get pulled across eastern Sonora and \neventually southwest TX. Still looking like the bulk of the \nheavy rain through Tue will be from Western KS through Central \nKS and into eastern NE. This is where 850mb convergence zone \nwill be situated, and with PW values around or slightly over 2 \ninches, heavy rain and flooding will be likely with the stronger\nactivity. Normal PW values for this time of year are around \n0.85 in. \n\nUpper low and associated vort max will start to move out across\nthe High Plains on Wed afternoon into Wed evening. The late Wed\nnight through Thu time frame is when it looks like all of the \nforecast will have the best chance to see widespread heavy rain,\nas the front slowly works through the area. For Thu night into \nSat, the upper low looks to get cut off over Northern TX/TX \nPanhandle, which will keep pops in for southern and southeast \nKS.",
